IMPORT prelude
IMPORT daydate
IMPORT `cga-types`

-- Consumer Guarantees Act 1993 — GOALS 2 AND 3: GOODS.
--
--   "Is the supplier, or the manufacturer, answerable for this failure of the
--    goods; is the failure of a substantial character; may the consumer
--    still reject the goods; which remedies are open; and how much are the
--    damages for the reduction in value?"
--
-- Source: ../registers/source-bundle/act_public_1993_91.txt — Parts 1-3,
-- ss 5-27, 42, 44.

§ `Goals 2 and 3 — goods`

§§ `Redress against the supplier (ss 5A, 7B, 11, 16, 17)`

-- s 16 "This Part gives a consumer a right of redress against a supplier of
-- goods where the goods fail to comply with any guarantee set out in any of
-- sections 5 to 10." s 7B(2): Part 2 applies to s 7A "as if gas and
-- electricity were goods, and as if gas retailers and electricity retailers
-- were the suppliers". s 11(2)-(3) the price guarantee has its own redress.
-- s 17: no redress against the supplier for acceptable quality where the
-- goods would have complied but for a representation by the manufacturer
-- (not on packaging or a label).
@ref ss 7B(2), 11, 16, 17
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the supplier is answerable under Part 2 for` g IF
    CONSIDER g's `guarantee failed`
    WHEN `s 5 title`                                   THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 5A delivery`                               THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 6 acceptable quality`                      THEN NOT g's `fails acceptable quality only because of a manufacturer's representation`
    WHEN `s 7A acceptable quality of gas or electricity` THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 8 fitness for a particular purpose`        THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 9 correspondence with description`         THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 10 correspondence with sample`             THEN TRUE
    WHEN `s 11 price`                                  THEN FALSE
    WHEN `s 12 repairs and spare parts`                THEN FALSE
    WHEN `s 13 a manufacturer's express guarantee`     THEN FALSE

-- s 21 "a failure to comply with a guarantee is of a substantial character in
-- any case where— (a) the goods would not have been acquired by a reasonable
-- consumer fully acquainted with the nature and extent of the failure; or (b)
-- the goods depart in 1 or more significant respects from the description
-- ... or ... from the sample or demonstration model; or (c) the goods are
-- substantially unfit for a purpose for which goods of the type in question
-- are commonly supplied or, where section 8(1) applies, ... unfit for a
-- particular purpose ..., and the goods cannot easily and within a
-- reasonable time be remedied ...; or (d) the goods are not of acceptable
-- quality within the meaning of section 7 because they are unsafe."
@ref s 21
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the goods failure is of a substantial character` g IF
        g's `a reasonable consumer fully acquainted with the failure would not have acquired the goods`
    OR  g's `the goods depart in a significant respect from the description, sample or model`
    OR  g's `substantially unfit for a common or made-known purpose, and not easily and quickly remedied`
    OR  g's `not of acceptable quality because unsafe`

-- s 20(1) "The right to reject goods ... shall not apply if— (a) the right is
-- not exercised within a reasonable time ...; or (b) the goods have been
-- disposed of by the consumer, or have been lost or destroyed while in the
-- possession of a person other than the supplier or an agent of the
-- supplier; or (c) the goods were damaged after delivery to the consumer for
-- reasons not related to their state or condition at the time of supply; or
-- (d) the goods have been attached to or incorporated in any real or
-- personal property and they cannot be detached or isolated without
-- damaging them."
@ref s 20
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the right to reject still applies to` g IF
        g's `rejected within a reasonable time`
    AND NOT g's `disposed of, or lost or destroyed away from the supplier`
    AND NOT g's `damaged after delivery for reasons unrelated to their state at supply`
    AND NOT g's `attached to property and cannot be detached without damage`

-- s 18 "(2) Where the failure can be remedied, the consumer may— (a) require
-- the supplier to remedy the failure within a reasonable time ...: (b) where
-- a supplier who has been required to remedy a failure refuses or neglects
-- to do so, or does not succeed in doing so within a reasonable time,— (i)
-- have the failure remedied elsewhere and obtain from the supplier all
-- reasonable costs ...; or (ii) subject to section 20, reject the goods ...
-- (3) Where the failure cannot be remedied or is of a substantial character
-- ..., the consumer may— (a) subject to section 20, reject the goods ...; or
-- (b) obtain from the supplier damages in compensation for any reduction in
-- value of the goods below the price paid or payable ... (4) In addition
-- ..., the consumer may obtain from the supplier damages for any loss or
-- damage ... which was reasonably foreseeable". FORK F1: a failure that can
-- be remedied but is of a substantial character opens both (2) and (3); a
-- remedy open under both is listed once.
-- s 5A(2), (4): for delivery, only rejection (if the failure is
-- substantial) and (4) damages. s 11(2)-(3): for price, only refusing to pay
-- more than a reasonable price. s 23: on rejection, a refund or a
-- replacement at the consumer's choice.
@ref ss 5A(2), 11(2), 18, 20, 21, 23
@export The remedies a consumer has against the supplier for a failure of goods under ss 16-23 of the Consumer Guarantees Act 1993 (NZ): if the failure can be remedied, require the supplier to remedy it and, if the supplier refuses, neglects or fails, have it remedied elsewhere at the supplier's cost or reject the goods; if it cannot be remedied or is of a substantial character, reject the goods (for a refund or replacement) or claim damages for the reduction in value; always, reasonably foreseeable consequential loss. Rejection is lost under s 20. Delivery and price failures have only their own remedies (ss 5A, 11). DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`  @desc The guarantee failed, whether the failure can be remedied and whether the supplier refused or failed to, the s 21 facts, the s 20 facts, the s 17 representation, and whether there is foreseeable consequential loss
GIVETH A LIST OF Remedy
`the remedies against the supplier for` g MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF g's `guarantee failed` EQUALS `s 11 price`
            THEN LIST `refuse to pay more than a reasonable price`
        IF NOT `the supplier is answerable under Part 2 for` g
            THEN EMPTY
        IF g's `guarantee failed` EQUALS `s 5A delivery`
            THEN append (IF substantial AND mayReject THEN LIST `reject the goods and choose a refund or a replacement` ELSE EMPTY) consequential
        OTHERWISE
            nub (append (append fromTwo fromThree) consequential)
    WHERE
        substantial MEANS `the goods failure is of a substantial character` g
        mayReject   MEANS `the right to reject still applies to` g
        remediable  MEANS g's `the failure can be remedied`
        refused     MEANS g's `the supplier refused, neglected or failed to remedy it`
        fromTwo MEANS
            IF   remediable
            THEN append (LIST `require the supplier to remedy the failure within a reasonable time`)
                        (IF   refused
                         THEN append (LIST `have the failure remedied elsewhere and recover the reasonable costs from the supplier`)
                                     (IF mayReject THEN LIST `reject the goods and choose a refund or a replacement` ELSE EMPTY)
                         ELSE EMPTY)
            ELSE EMPTY
        fromThree MEANS
            IF   (NOT remediable) OR substantial
            THEN append (IF mayReject THEN LIST `reject the goods and choose a refund or a replacement` ELSE EMPTY)
                        (LIST `damages from the supplier for the reduction in value`)
            ELSE EMPTY
        consequential MEANS
            IF g's `there is reasonably foreseeable consequential loss` THEN LIST `damages from the supplier for reasonably foreseeable consequential loss` ELSE EMPTY

-- s 18(3)(b) "damages in compensation for any reduction in value of the
-- goods below the price paid or payable by the consumer for the goods".
@ref s 18(3)(b)
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the supplier's damages for the reduction in value of` g MEANS
    max 0 (g's `price paid or payable` MINUS g's `value of the goods with the failure`)

§§ `Redress against the manufacturer (ss 12-14, 25-27, 42)`

-- s 25 "This Part gives a consumer a right of redress against a manufacturer
-- of goods where— (a) the goods fail to comply with the guarantee as to
-- acceptable quality set out in section 6: (b) the goods fail to comply with
-- the guarantee as to correspondence with description ... due to the
-- failure of the goods to correspond with any description applied to the
-- goods by or on behalf of the manufacturer or with the express or implied
-- consent of the manufacturer: (c) the goods fail to comply with the
-- guarantee as to repairs and parts set out in section 12: (d) the goods
-- fail, during the currency of the guarantee, to comply with any express
-- guarantee given by the manufacturer that is binding on the manufacturer in
-- accordance with section 14." s 26 the exceptions; s 42 a notice that
-- repairs and parts will not be available.
@ref ss 12, 14, 25, 26, 42
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `the manufacturer is answerable under Part 3 for` g IF
    CONSIDER g's `guarantee failed`
    WHEN `s 6 acceptable quality` THEN
            NOT g's `fails only because of another person or a cause after the goods left the manufacturer`
        AND NOT g's `fails acceptable quality only because the price was above the recommended or average retail price`
    WHEN `s 9 correspondence with description` THEN
            g's `the description was applied by or with the consent of the manufacturer`
        AND NOT g's `fails only because of another person or a cause after the goods left the manufacturer`
    WHEN `s 12 repairs and spare parts`            THEN NOT g's `notice under s 42 excludes s 12`
    WHEN `s 13 a manufacturer's express guarantee` THEN g's `the express guarantee binds the manufacturer and was current`
    OTHERWISE FALSE

-- s 27(1) "(a) ... for any reduction in the value of the goods resulting from
-- the failure— (i) below the price paid or payable by the consumer for the
-- goods; or (ii) below the average retail price of the goods at the time of
-- supply,— whichever price is lower: (b) for any loss or damage ... which
-- was reasonably foreseeable". (2) Where an express guarantee entitles the
-- consumer to require the manufacturer to repair or replace, "no action shall
-- be commenced under subsection (1)(a) unless the consumer ... has required
-- the manufacturer to remedy the failure and the manufacturer— (a) has either
-- refused or neglected to remedy the failure; or (b) has not succeeded in
-- remedying the failure within a reasonable time."
@ref s 27
@export The remedies a consumer has against the manufacturer under ss 25-27 of the Consumer Guarantees Act 1993 (NZ): for a failure of acceptable quality, of a description the manufacturer applied, of repairs and parts, or of a current express guarantee (subject to the s 26 exceptions and a s 42 notice), damages for the reduction in value (only after requiring repair or replacement first, where an express guarantee provides for it) and for reasonably foreseeable consequential loss. DRAFT, not reviewed by a New Zealand lawyer.
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`  @desc The guarantee failed, who applied the description, the s 26 causes, any s 42 notice, the express guarantee and whether the manufacturer was asked to remedy and did not, and whether there is foreseeable consequential loss
GIVETH A LIST OF Remedy
`the remedies against the manufacturer for` g MEANS
    IF   `the manufacturer is answerable under Part 3 for` g
    THEN append
            (IF   (NOT g's `an express guarantee entitles the consumer to repair or replacement`)
                  OR g's `the manufacturer refused, neglected or failed to remedy it`
             THEN LIST `damages from the manufacturer for the reduction in value`
             ELSE EMPTY)
            (IF g's `there is reasonably foreseeable consequential loss` THEN LIST `damages from the manufacturer for reasonably foreseeable consequential loss` ELSE EMPTY)
    ELSE EMPTY

@ref s 27(1)(a)
GIVEN g IS A `Goods failure`
GIVETH A NUMBER
`the manufacturer's damages for the reduction in value of` g MEANS
    max 0 ((min (g's `price paid or payable`) (g's `average retail price at the time of supply`)) MINUS g's `value of the goods with the failure`)
